<p>Spotted some strange word choices and other miscellaneous issues.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>If possible, the original copies of these instances are to be deleted from the website's server with the <strong>explanation</strong> of removing infectious malware.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Did you mean purpose? I've never seen 'explanation' used in this context before.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-3131 instances most commonly occur on websites hosting illegal free downloads of copyrighted music.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is perhaps just a stylistic nitpick, but examples of such websites (Pirate's Bay, UTorrent) would make this feel more like a professional document IMO.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is noted that while SCP-3131 instances can be read by any suitable media player, doing so requires consumption of nearly ten times the usual amount of memory necessary to play non-anomalous music files.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ehh, I don't quite understand why or what the purpose of this is. Again, this is heavy nitpicking, but if we're going under the assumption that these are spirits trapped in MP3 files, are we expected to believe that ghosts use up more memory or something? That just seems a bit silly, and feels thrown in to back up the idea of the data corrupting. Because corrupted data is spooky, I guess?</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Interview Log 3131-275</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>This felt pretty underwhelming. I'm not sure if Researcher Versova is dim or just really grasping at straws. For example, here:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>SCP-3131-275:</strong> The coffee. I am. The coffee.</p>
<p><strong>Researcher Versoza:</strong> Your name is &quot;coffee&quot;?</p>
<p><strong>SCP-3131-275:</strong> Only halfway.</p>
<p><strong>Researcher Versoza:</strong> Did you name yourself &quot;coffee&quot; or you're named &quot;coffee&quot;?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't know if this was intended, but doesn't it feel a bit childish? The tone throughout just feels very quick and impatient, as if the Researcher doesn't really care about what the instance has to say. I mean, you've created a personality, I'll commend you on that, but I don't think it's the one you were looking for. Also, as an offhand note, why does the 3131 instance say:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>SCP-3131-275:</strong> Only halfway.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230; which would imply that 'coffee' isn't its full name, but then just accept the name anyway? Doesn't that make this line completely unnecessary? I just feel as though the interview really pales in comparison to the chilling nature of the Observational Log, and that takes the punch right out of the article as a whole, considering they're the two largest blocks of character development we have available as a reader.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Addendum 3131-2:</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Cute, but it doesn't really make much sense, unless you're implying that the files live in some sort of Toy Story-esque world in which when the computers are off they're just normal people living in houses and buildings. That's a very heavy thing to imply for what is generally a shorter skip.<br />
No comment on the following Addenda, as it's just a copy-and-paste of my previous criticisms.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I don't know about this one. The quote '<a href="https://allpoetry.com/The-Hollow-Men">This is the way the world ends. Not with a bang, but a whimper.</a>' comes to mind in that the article had an undeniably strong premise and start, but waned towards the end and ultimately failed to deliver on what it had set up prior. It's almost like two entirely separate articles were spliced together. I can definitely feel Zyn's influence towards the end, and I think that's where the problem lies. Zyn is an excellent writer, but her articles are so great because they are charming and bubbly <em>throughout</em>. When you take something initially quite haunting and then try to make it equally charming and/or bubbly&#8230; ehh? It just feels weak.</p>
<p>I was going to give a rather tentative upvote, but upon further consideration I'm just going to hit the downvote. Ultimately, it's a very interesting idea with a good jump-start, but felt bored with itself as soon as the Observation Log ended. Best of luck in your future projects, but this feels like lost potential.</p>

							</div>

								
