<p>Good points.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>no, it was meant to indicate that the explanation was given as a cover story.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Duh-doy, of course it was. Apologies, I misread.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>indicate that the anomalous .mp3s were inherently different</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That doesn't really need to be reinforced.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the sapience reflects in the memory usage.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That still doesn't really make any sense, but in fairness this is only going by a traditional understanding of spirits/ghosts/etc. It's the Foundation, so I can suspend my disbelief a little.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>could you tell us what the haunting bits were?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Just the general concept of 'digital sapience talks through song lyrics' produced quite an unsettling image. This Frankenstein's Monster of clips strung together and repeated.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>incidentally, I didn't write the interview or the addenda</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's very interesting. It doesn't really change my stance, but I would have sworn blind on having a lot of input towards the end. It felt very similar to your writing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Can you suggest ways to spin that material into something that suits the observational log?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1722371&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1504474857" alt="Shaggydredlocks" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1722371)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;">Shaggydredlocks</a></span> pointed out, there's a loose end in the concept of this 'mystery woman' which I think would benefit from a bit more explanation. It nails the 'don't show your monster' feel, or at least hints at some kind of heart-wrenching past, but falls flat without any proper follow-up.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I think if you'd gone from extreme uneasiness to extreme tragedy<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-243270-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-243270-1')">1</a></sup>, it would have been more effective. Sort of like the breakneck turn of emotions in <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-2006">SCP-2006</a>. We're led to believe it's going to be another fairly lighthearted, goofy skip, but then we're shot right into another powerful feeling: dread. As it stands, you have this strong feeling of uneasiness followed by a slow dither, and it actually makes the switch more jarring as a result.</p>
<p>I hope that makes sense. For what it's worth, this certainly isn't a <em>bad</em> article, it's just very underwhelming.</p>
