<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Just the general concept of 'digital sapience talks through song lyrics' produced quite an unsettling image. This Frankenstein's Monster of clips strung together and repeated.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's the core idea actually. I was really planning on including an additional interview log regarding a supposed &quot;mp3 cannibal&quot;, or possibly even a direct interview with this Frankenstein mp3 cannibal thing, eventually makinh a complex conversation through the diversity of music it devoured. That was from the original idea that these mp3s all have the capacity to affect and copy other digital audio to themselves, as if they can see the &quot;audio stream&quot; of each other, the same way they can perceive human speech. I decided to put it beside for now because it may be a very complex thing to do for such inexperienced writer like me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That still doesn't really make any sense, but in fairness this is only going by a traditional understanding of spirits/ghosts/etc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>One of the stuff I had in mind was that most of the instances where recovered during murders, and the search was only done recently due to the interests of Foundation researchers.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We're led to believe it's going to be another fairly lighthearted, goofy skip, but then we're shot right into another powerful feeling: dread.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Same with Zyn, I wasn't really planning to make the reader feel dread about these mp3s, though I'm sure it'll happen anyway because no matter how much the Foundation researchers seem amused and fascinated about them, seeing their complex behavior, the thought of their origins and the underlying meaning behind their twisted lyrics will put anyone directly to the uncanny valley.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I know 'tragedy' perhaps isn't the intended response here, but this is just assuming you did expand upon this 'mystery woman' idea.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The mystery woman, I'll disclose, is just about how it might've used it's lyrics to evade an interview. It's like, it knows that scaring the researcher may make the interview easier or just make it seem dubious or nonsensical in contextual meaning.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>I don't know if this was intended, but doesn't it feel a bit childish? The tone throughout just feels very quick and impatient, as if the Researcher doesn't really care about what the instance has to say.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think the researcher really wasn't expecting a productive interview. Like what's mentioned from the article, it was the first successful interview, and that doesn't necessarily mean it was meant to be successful. It might imply the other interviews were conducted this short and simple, as they were still experimenting for a better mode of communication.</p>
</div>
</div>
